{
  "abstract": "The norm-and-anchor surface of a determinant-zero Diophantine septuple chart is a known Kummer surface attached to the square of E: y²=x(x−1)(x−2). We classify two geometric mechanisms for imposing its additional splitting character. For every nonzero rational d and every genus-one source with two nonconstant rational maps to the common twist d y²=x(x−1)(x−2), a square splitting character forces the image to be the diagonal or one two-torsion translate; both reconstruct degenerate tuples. Neither a rational source point nor a restriction on the twist's rational rank is required. A translation-independent contact bound reduces arbitrary multiplication degrees and non-torsion translations to an exact finite remainder. A quantitative version bounds the genus of the splitting cover. For commensurable maps to the untwisted E from sources of genus at most four, only one further image occurs; its rational splitting points are excluded by a rank-zero conductor-56 curve. We also determine the full rational Mordell–Weil group after the first halving-conic base change of the alternate Kummer fibration and classify every square-valued splitting section. Exactly six survive, all degenerate; the two new half sections trace the translated diagonal. A real elliptic-logarithm obstruction bounds all coefficients, followed by exact residue tests. Author and independent certificates support the divisor calculations, translation classification, group computation and reconstruction. Higher-genus sources on arbitrary twists, noncommensurable maps, further base changes and rational points on the full surface remain open.",
